      "files": {
        "0": {
          "id": "0",
          "name": "main.py",
          "language": "py",
          "contents": "# CIW AI Associate Standard: 2.6\n# Standard Description: Explain the importance of feature engineering.\n#\n# Rationale: Sometimes data comes in different \"sizes\". For example, Age (0-100) \n# and Salary (20,000 - 200,000). If we don't fix this, the AI might think \n# Salary is more important just because the numbers are bigger! \n# This tutorial shows how \"Scaling\" helps.\n\n\"\"\"\nWelcome! Have you ever tried to compare Apples to Oranges? \nIn AI, we often have data that looks very different. \n- One column might be \"Years of Experience\" (small numbers like 1, 5, 10).\n- Another might be \"Annual Salary\" (huge numbers like 50000, 100000).\n\nIf we aren't careful, the AI will ignore the small numbers. \nWe fix this with \"Feature Scaling\".\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_wine\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# --- LOAD DATA ---\n# The Wine dataset has features with very different scales (e.g., Proline vs Alcohol)\nwine = load_wine()\nX, y = wine.data, wine.target\n\n# We'll use just two features to make it easy to visualize:\n# Feature 0: Alcohol (looks like 11.0 - 14.0)\n# Feature 12: Proline (looks like 200 - 1600)\nX_subset = X[:, [0, 12]]\n\n# Split the data\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X_subset, y, random_state=42)\n\n# --- PART 1: WITHOUT SCALING ---\nprint(\"--- Part 1: Training WITHOUT Scaling ---\")\nknn_raw = KNeighborsClassifier()\nknn_raw.fit(X_train, y_train)\nscore_raw = knn_raw.score(X_test, y_test)\nprint(f\"Accuracy with raw data: {score_raw:.2%}\")\n\n# --- PART 2: WITH SCALING ---\nprint(\"--- Part 2: Training WITH Scaling ---\")\n# StandardScaler shifts the data so the mean is 0 and the scale is 1.\nscaler = StandardScaler()\nX_train_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)\nX_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test) # Note: We use .transform() on test data, not .fit_transform()!\n\nknn_scaled = KNeighborsClassifier()\nknn_scaled.fit(X_train_scaled, y_train)\nscore_scaled = knn_scaled.score(X_test_scaled, y_test)\nprint(f\"Accuracy with scaled data: {score_scaled:.2%}\")\n\nprint(\"Notice the improvement! The AI can now 'see' both features equally.\")\n\n# --- VISUALIZATION ---\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\n\n# Raw Data Plot\nplt.subplot(1, 2, 1)\nplt.scatter(X_train[:, 0], X_train[:, 1], c=y_train, cmap='viridis', edgecolors='k')\nplt.title(\"Raw Data (Look at the axes!)\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Alcohol\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Proline\")\n\n# Scaled Data Plot\nplt.subplot(1, 2, 2)\nplt.scatter(X_train_scaled[:, 0], X_train_scaled[:, 1], c=y_train, cmap='viridis', edgecolors='k')\nplt.title(\"Scaled Data (Centered at 0)\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Alcohol (Scaled)\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Proline (Scaled)\")\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\n\"\"\"\n--- YOUR TURN ---\nWe've loaded the 'Breast Cancer' dataset for you. \n1. Train a KNeighborsClassifier on the raw data and print the score.\n2. Scale the data using StandardScaler.\n3. Train a new KNeighborsClassifier on the scaled data and print the score.\n\"\"\"\n\n# STARTING CODE FOR STUDENT:\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_breast_cancer\n\ndata = load_breast_cancer()\nX_bc = data.data\ny_bc = data.target\n\nX_train_bc, X_test_bc, y_train_bc, y_test_bc = train_test_split(X_bc, y_bc, random_state=42)\n\n# TODO: Train and score on RAW data\n\n# TODO: Create a scaler, fit_transform the train data, and transform the test data\n\n# TODO: Train and score on SCALED data\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": true
        }
